window.KB_EXPLAIN_EN_ADD({"L0-0.1.1":"The bias-variance decomposition splits a model's expected error into three parts. It tells you whether the model is underfitting or overfitting, and therefore whether the next step is to add capacity or add constraints.\n\n**How it works**\nUnder squared loss: `E[(y − f̂(x))²] = Bias² + Var + σ²`, where σ² is irreducible noise. Diagnose with learning curves:\n- Training and validation error both high and close together → high bias: add features, use a stronger model, weaken regularization.\n- Low training error, high validation error → high variance: add data, add regularization, early stopping or bagging.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nModern deep networks exhibit double descent: once the parameter count passes the interpolation threshold (the point where the model can exactly fit all training data), test error can drop again. So \"more parameters always means overfitting\" is false; what matters is effective capacity and implicit regularization (SGD noise, data augmentation). The classic U-shaped curve only describes the region before the interpolation threshold.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Always name the two diagnostic signals: learning curves and the train–validation error gap.\n- If asked \"has deep learning broken this trade-off?\" → answer with double descent + implicit regularization, not a flat \"it doesn't apply\".\n- With high bias, adding more regularization only makes things worse.","L0-0.1.2":"L1 and L2 are the two most basic parameter-norm penalties. Both reduce variance by constraining the weights, but L1 produces sparse solutions while L2 only shrinks weights overall.\n\n**How it works**\n- L2 (ridge / weight decay): penalizes `‖w‖²`; weights shrink smoothly toward 0 but rarely hit exactly 0. The Bayesian view is a Gaussian prior.\n- L1 (lasso): penalizes `‖w‖₁`; non-differentiable at zero, corresponding to a Laplace prior.\n- Why L1 is sparse: geometrically, the L1 constraint region is a diamond, and the loss contours usually touch it first at a vertex on a coordinate axis. In optimization terms, the L1 proximal operator is soft-thresholding, which sets `|w| < λ` straight to 0. From the subgradient view, L1's subgradient at 0 is the interval `[−λ, λ]`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nL1 can do feature selection, but among correlated features it picks one more or less at random, which is unstable; use Elastic Net in that case. Under Adam, L2 cannot simply be added to the gradient (it gets rescaled by the adaptive step size); use AdamW with decoupled weight decay instead.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Give both the geometric (diamond vertices) and Bayesian (MAP + Laplace prior) explanations.\n- Follow-up \"what if features are correlated?\" → Elastic Net.\n- Bonus: Adam + L2 ≠ weight decay, which is why AdamW exists.","L0-0.1.3":"Beyond L1 / L2 there are many ways to reduce variance. All of them either limit effective capacity or inject noise into training; interviews tend to probe how their mechanisms differ and when to pick each.\n\n**How it works**\n- Elastic Net: L1 + L2; keeps correlated features together as a group, fixing lasso's unstable habit of picking one at random.\n- Early stopping: limits the effective number of training steps; under gradient descent it approximates L2 regularization.\n- Dropout: during training, zero activations with probability p and scale by 1/(1−p) (inverted dropout); turn it off at inference. It can be seen as an approximate ensemble. Large-model pretraining often sets it to 0.\n- Data augmentation, label smoothing: inject noise or smooth the target distribution to prevent overconfidence.\n- Tree-model constraints: max_depth, min_child_weight, γ, subsample.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe right choice depends on the failure mode: add regularization or data only when the train–validation gap is large; when both training and validation error are high, more regularization makes things worse. Deep models usually combine weight decay, dropout, data augmentation and label smoothing.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"What happens to dropout at inference?\" → it is turned off; during training, activations are scaled by 1/(1−p) so the expectation is unchanged.\n- Be able to state that early stopping ≈ L2.\n- Pick techniques based on the diagnosis rather than listing them all.","L0-0.1.4":"The loss function determines which statistic the model actually optimizes. Choose it to match the task type, the noise distribution and the data (outliers, class imbalance, representation learning).\n\n**How it works**\n- Regression: MSE `(y−ŷ)²` suits roughly Gaussian noise but is sensitive to outliers; MAE `|y−ŷ|` predicts the conditional median and is robust to outliers, but its gradient is discontinuous at 0; Huber is quadratic for small errors and linear for large ones, getting the best of both, with a threshold δ to tune; quantile / pinball `max(τe, (τ−1)e)` gives prediction intervals, with τ = 0.9 corresponding to P90.\n- Classification: cross-entropy `−Σ y_c log p_c`, whose gradient with softmax / sigmoid is `p − y`; focal loss `−(1−p_t)^γ log p_t` down-weights easy examples, used for extreme imbalance and detection; hinge `max(0, 1 − y·f(x))` only cares about samples inside the margin, corresponding to SVM.\n- Representation learning: InfoNCE `−log(exp(s⁺/τ)/Σexp(s/τ))`, used in CLIP and two-tower models; the temperature τ and the number of negatives matter a lot.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe loss affects not only accuracy but what the output means: MSE estimates the mean, MAE the median, pinball a quantile.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- With outliers, pick MAE / Huber; for demand forecasting that needs intervals, use quantile loss.\n- The CE + softmax gradient is p − y, which is why it trains so well.\n- InfoNCE temperature and negative count are common follow-ups.","L0-0.1.5":"The optimizer decides how gradients update parameters. In interviews you should be able to explain how first-order methods differ and where second-order methods fit in classical ML.\n\n**How it works**\n- SGD + momentum: accumulates past gradient directions; usually generalizes well and is still common in CV.\n- Adam: uses first- and second-moment estimates to adapt the step size per parameter; insensitive to hyperparameters.\n- AdamW: decouples weight decay from the gradient and applies it directly to the weights; the default for Transformers.\n- Learning-rate schedule: usually warmup + cosine / linear decay.\n- Second-order methods: L-BFGS is the default solver for sklearn `LogisticRegression`; Newton's method is the idea behind XGBoost's second-order Taylor expansion (gradient g and Hessian h).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAdaptive methods converge fast and need less tuning, though some argue they generalize slightly worse than SGD. Second-order methods extract more information per step, but the cost of computing and storing the Hessian limits them to models with relatively few parameters. Adding L2 directly under Adam gets distorted by the adaptive step size, hence AdamW.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why AdamW rather than Adam + L2?\" → decoupled weight decay.\n- Connect XGBoost to Newton's method.\n- What warmup does: early in training Adam's second-moment estimates are unstable, so warmup avoids diverging with large steps.","L0-0.1.6":"The costs a business actually cares about are often asymmetric (missing a fraudulent transaction vs. blocking a legitimate one), but such business metrics are usually non-differentiable. The standard approach is two steps: \"train with a surrogate loss + decide with a business threshold\".\n\n**How it works**\n1. Train the model with a differentiable, well-behaved surrogate loss (e.g. cross-entropy) to get the best possible ranking and probabilities.\n2. On the validation set, sweep thresholds against the cost matrix (FP cost, FN cost) and pick the operating point with the lowest expected business loss.\n3. If probabilities feed directly into decisions (e.g. expected loss = p × amount), calibrate first, then choose the threshold.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nHard-coding the business metric into the loss is usually non-differentiable and hard to optimize, and it ties the model to the business rules of one moment in time. With the two separated, a change in costs only requires picking a new threshold, not retraining. Tuning the threshold by cost is often the single highest-payoff step for imbalanced problems. The price is that you need a validation set matching the production distribution, and the threshold must be re-checked after distribution drift.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Give the two-stage answer: \"train with CE → pick the threshold on the validation set by cost\".\n- The threshold is an operational decision: agree with the business on a minimum recall or an acceptable false-positive volume.\n- When probability values are used directly, add \"calibrate first\".","L0-0.2.1":"Linear and logistic regression are the most classic linear models, one for regression and one for binary classification. They remain the baseline and the regulator-friendly choice in settings such as financial risk control.\n\n**How it works**\n- Linear regression: ridge closed form `w = (XᵀX + λI)⁻¹Xᵀy`; with many features or lots of data, switch to GD / SGD. Classic assumptions: linearity, independent errors, homoscedasticity, no severe multicollinearity (collinearity inflates coefficient variance and hurts interpretability, but may not hurt prediction).\n- Logistic regression: `p = σ(wᵀx + b)`, loss BCE. The gradient is clean: `∇w = Xᵀ(p − y)/n + λw`; the Hessian `Σ p(1−p)xxᵀ` is positive semidefinite, so the problem is convex.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTwo reasons not to use MSE: MSE on top of a sigmoid is non-convex (for a single sample with y=0, the second derivative is negative where σ > 2/3); and in saturation the MSE gradient carries a σ'(z) factor that goes to 0, stalling learning, whereas BCE cancels σ' so the gradient grows the more wrong the prediction is. LR minimizes log loss (a proper scoring rule), so its probabilities are naturally fairly well calibrated, and coefficients read as changes in log-odds.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Derive the gradient `Xᵀ(p−y)/n` by hand and prove convexity via the PSD Hessian.\n- \"Why not MSE?\" → non-convex + vanishing gradients in the saturated region.\n- On linearly separable data without regularization, the weights diverge.","L0-0.2.2":"Computing sigmoid and BCE straight from the definitions overflows or hits log(0) when logits are very large or very small, so production implementations must be made numerically stable.\n\n**How it works**\n- Stable sigmoid: handle z ≥ 0 and z < 0 separately. For z ≥ 0 use `1/(1+exp(−z))`; for z < 0 use `exp(z)/(1+exp(z))`. The argument to exp is then never a large positive number, avoiding overflow like exp(1000).\n- Stable BCE: use the identity `−[y log σ(z) + (1−y) log(1−σ(z))] = log(1+eᶻ) − yz` and compute `np.logaddexp(0, z) − y*z` directly on the logit, stable for any z. This is exactly what PyTorch's `BCEWithLogitsLoss` does.\n- Verification: run a gradient check with central differences (error below 1e-6), and check that a convex objective decreases monotonically with a small step size.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nComputing sigmoid first and then taking the log loses precision, or even yields inf / NaN, when probabilities approach 0 or 1; fusing sigmoid and log is both stable and saves a step. Likewise, for multiclass use log-softmax (log-sum-exp with the max subtracted) rather than softmax followed by log.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When hand-coding LR, proactively mention the overflow issue and the logaddexp formulation.\n- Be able to explain why `BCEWithLogitsLoss` beats `Sigmoid + BCELoss`.\n- Mention verifying the implementation with a gradient check.","L0-0.2.3":"SVMs find a decision boundary by maximizing the classification margin and handle nonlinear problems via the kernel trick. Today they mostly show up in small-sample, high-dimensional settings and in conceptual questions.\n\n**How it works**\n- Hard margin: `min ½‖w‖² s.t. yᵢ(wᵀxᵢ+b) ≥ 1`.\n- Soft margin is equivalent to `½‖w‖² + C Σ hinge`, with hinge = `max(0, 1 − y·f(x))`.\n- The dual form depends only on inner products `xᵢᵀxⱼ`, so they can be replaced by a kernel K (RBF, polynomial), solving implicitly in a high-dimensional feature space — that is the kernel trick.\n- The decision function is determined only by the support vectors.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLarger C penalizes misclassification more heavily and overfits more easily. Kernel SVM training costs roughly O(n²)–O(n³), so beyond ~100k samples it becomes impractical; it suits small-sample, high-dimensional settings (e.g. text TF-IDF). Compared with LR, SVM does not output probabilities directly (it needs Platt scaling for calibration) and only cares about samples near the margin.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the equivalence between the soft-margin objective and hinge loss.\n- Explain why the dual form makes the kernel trick possible.\n- Explain what C does and where the scale limit is.","L0-0.2.4":"A decision tree splits data recursively, each time choosing the feature and threshold that reduce impurity the most. It is the basic building block of random forests and GBDT.\n\n**How it works**\nFor each feature and each candidate threshold, compute the impurity decrease after the split and pick the largest:\n- Classification: Gini `1 − Σp²` or entropy `−Σp log p`.\n- Regression: SSE / variance.\nEfficient implementation: sort each feature once, scan all split points in one pass with cumulative sums, and never split between identical values.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPros: invariant to monotonic transformations, no normalization needed, captures feature interactions automatically, interpretable. Cons: a single tree has high variance; it has zero ability to extrapolate — a regression tree never predicts outside the range of training labels, which matters especially when extrapolating trends in time series. In practice trees are rarely used alone: bagging (random forest) reduces variance and boosting (GBDT) reduces bias.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State the split criterion for classification and for regression.\n- \"Can tree models extrapolate?\" → No; detrend trending data first or switch models.\n- Why no feature scaling is needed: splits only depend on the ordering of values.","L0-0.2.5":"Random forests and GBDT are the two mainstream tree ensembles, representing bagging and boosting respectively. The key differences are how they train and which error component they mainly reduce.\n\n**How it works**\n- Random forest (bagging): train many deep trees in parallel, each on row sampling (bootstrap) + column sampling, and average the results. Deep trees have low bias and high variance, so averaging mainly reduces variance.\n- GBDT (boosting): train shallow trees sequentially, each fitting the negative gradient of the current loss (the residual under squared loss). This mainly reduces bias, with shrinkage (the learning rate) keeping variance in check.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Tuning: random forests are easy and work well with defaults; GBDT needs the learning rate, number of trees and depth tuned.\n- Overfitting: random forests get more stable with more trees; GBDT overfits with too many trees and needs early stopping.\n- Use cases: random forests for quick baselines, OOB error estimates and feature importance; GBDT is the SOTA workhorse for tabular data.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One line: bagging = parallel deep trees reducing variance; boosting = sequential shallow trees reducing bias.\n- \"How do you prevent boosting from overfitting?\" → learning rate, tree depth, subsampling, early stopping.\n- \"GBDT or neural nets for tabular data?\" → for medium-sized tabular data start with GBDT; use neural nets for images, text, audio and very large datasets.","L0-0.2.6":"XGBoost uses a second-order Taylor expansion of the loss to derive closed-form solutions for each leaf's optimal weight and for the split gain, and writes regularization straight into the gain, giving built-in pruning.\n\n**How it works**\nLet gᵢ, hᵢ be each sample's first- and second-order gradients, and G, H their sums within a leaf:\n- Optimal leaf weight: `w* = −G/(H+λ)`.\n- Split gain: `Gain = ½[G_L²/(H_L+λ) + G_R²/(H_R+λ) − G²/(H+λ)] − γ`.\n- λ is the L2 penalty on leaf weights and γ the penalty per additional leaf, so splits with Gain < 0 are pruned.\n- Under squared loss h = 1, and the gain equals exactly half the SSE reduction.\n- Implementation: sort each feature once, use cumsum to get G_L, H_L to the left of every split point in one pass, get the right side by subtracting from the totals, and set positions between identical values to −inf.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSecond-order information makes each update Newton-like and works for any twice-differentiable loss; λ and γ unify regularization and structure selection in a single formula. The cost is computing the Hessian, plus sensitivity to samples with very small h (λ smooths this out).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to derive w* and Gain and explain what λ and γ mean.\n- State that under squared loss, Gain = half the SSE reduction.\n- Contrast with plain GBDT, which uses only first-order gradients.","L0-0.2.7":"XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost are the three major gradient boosting libraries. All implement GBDT; they differ mainly in how trees grow, how splits are found and how categorical features are handled.\n\n**How it works**\n- XGBoost: depth-wise growth by default (lossguide also supported), hist binning by default, mature GPU support; good for general use, distributed training (Spark / Dask) and out-of-core training.\n- LightGBM: leaf-wise growth (always split the leaf with the largest gain), faster but more prone to overfitting, so cap num_leaves; histograms + GOSS (keep large-gradient samples, subsample small-gradient ones) + EFB (exclusive feature bundling); good for large data and training speed.\n- CatBoost: symmetric (oblivious) trees, with the same split condition across each level, giving very fast inference; categorical features are its strongest point, using ordered target statistics + ordered boosting to mitigate target leakage and prediction shift; good when there are many high-cardinality categorical features and you want little tuning.\n- Versions: XGBoost enables categorical features by default from 3.3.0; LightGBM is currently 4.7.0; CatBoost is 1.2.x.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLeaf-wise growth lowers the loss faster for the same number of leaves but overfits easily on small data; symmetric trees give up some expressiveness for extremely low inference latency.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One keyword set each: depth-wise; leaf-wise + GOSS + EFB; symmetric trees + ordered TS.\n- Explain why GOSS doesn't lose much accuracy: small-gradient samples are already well fit.\n- Recommend by scenario (distributed, speed, categorical features).","L0-0.2.8":"In production, tree models need attention beyond accuracy: interpretability, distribution drift, inference latency and compliance constraints. These are what separate \"can call the library\" from \"has shipped it\" in interviews.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Feature importance: gain / split count is biased toward high-cardinality features; for explaining the model, use SHAP or permutation importance.\n2. Drift monitoring: tree models don't extrapolate beyond the training distribution, so monitor feature drift.\n3. Inference latency: roughly number of trees × depth; online serving often uses Treelite, ONNX or the library's own C API, and CatBoost's symmetric trees suit low latency better.\n4. Monotonic constraints: e.g. \"higher income should not mean higher default probability\"; all three libraries support monotone constraints, commonly used for risk-control compliance.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe wrong importance measure leads the business to wrong conclusions. Monotonic constraints may cost a little offline accuracy, but they buy interpretable, auditable model behavior — a hard requirement in regulated industries such as finance.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Can you trust the default feature importance?\" → it is biased toward high-cardinality features; use SHAP / permutation instead.\n- In risk-control scenarios, bring up monotonic constraints proactively.\n- Latency optimization: fewer and shallower trees, compiled inference (Treelite / ONNX).","L0-0.2.9":"k-means is the most widely used clustering algorithm. It alternates between \"assign to the nearest center\" and \"set each center to the mean\" to minimize the within-cluster sum of squares; when clusters are not spherical, switch to GMM or DBSCAN.\n\n**How it works**\n- Lloyd iterations: neither step can increase the objective, and there are finitely many assignments, so it always converges — but only to a local optimum. Why the mean: with assignments fixed, setting the derivative of `Σ‖xᵢ−c‖²` w.r.t. c to 0 gives c = mean, and the Hessian `2nI` is positive definite.\n- k-means++: initialize by sampling with probability proportional to D(x)², the squared distance to the nearest chosen center; expected approximation ratio O(log k).\n- Implementation details: compute distances as `‖x‖² − 2x·c + ‖c‖²` to avoid building large tensors; re-seed an empty cluster at the point farthest from existing centers.\n- Choosing k: elbow method, silhouette, BIC for GMM — but the most reliable criterion is business usefulness.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nk-means assumes roughly spherical clusters of similar size and is sensitive to scale and outliers. GMM uses EM to give soft assignments and fits elliptical clusters; DBSCAN / HDBSCAN are density-based, find arbitrarily shaped clusters and noise points, but eps is hard to tune and they break down in high dimensions; hierarchical clustering needs O(n²) memory. k-means is also used for vector quantization (IVF coarse clustering, PQ codebooks).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Derive why the center is the mean.\n- With L1 distance the center becomes the median (k-medians); for cosine similarity use spherical k-means.\n- Initialization + multiple restarts, keep the best.","L0-0.2.10":"Dimensionality reduction is used for compression, decorrelation and visualization. PCA is linear and can transform new data directly; t-SNE and UMAP are nonlinear methods that preserve local neighborhoods, mainly used for visualization.\n\n**How it works**\n- PCA: take the SVD of centered data, `X_c = USVᵀ`; the principal components are the columns of V and the variances are `S²/(n−1)`, consistent with the eigendecomposition of the covariance matrix; projected coordinates are uncorrelated. Common bug: forgetting to center, so the first principal direction is pulled toward the mean.\n- t-SNE: preserves local neighborhoods via KL divergence; it cannot natively transform new data and is for visualization only. Distances between clusters and cluster sizes in the plot mean nothing.\n- UMAP: preserves local neighborhoods plus more global structure, is fast, can transform new data, and is often used for dimensionality reduction before clustering (e.g. BERTopic).\n- Autoencoder: nonlinear compression, also usable for anomaly detection (reconstruction error).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPCA only captures linear structure but is stable, interpretable and invertible; t-SNE / UMAP reveal nonlinear cluster structure but results depend on hyperparameters and cannot be used to quantify distances between clusters.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- PCA requires centering first; explain the relationship between SVD and covariance eigendecomposition.\n- \"What does it mean when two clusters are far apart in a t-SNE plot?\" → nothing.\n- When you need to handle new data, choose PCA or UMAP.","L0-0.3.1":"Precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC and PR-AUC are the core classification metrics. The key is understanding how differently they respond to class ratio.\n\n**How it works**\n- Precision = TP/(TP+FP), Recall = TP/(TP+FN), F1 is their harmonic mean; in Fβ, β > 1 weights recall more.\n- ROC-AUC: the probability that a random positive scores higher than a random negative; random baseline 0.5; invariant to class ratio because TPR and FPR are each normalized within their class. It can be computed in O(n log n) with the Mann–Whitney rank formula `AUC = (R₊ − n₊(n₊+1)/2)/(n₊n₋)`, using average ranks for ties.\n- PR-AUC (Average Precision): weighted average of precision across recall levels; random baseline equals the positive rate (e.g. 0.1%), so it is sensitive to class ratio.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nExperiment: duplicate every negative 10×, and ROC-AUC is completely unchanged while AP drops noticeably — FPR's denominator grows with the negatives, whereas the absolute number of FPs in precision goes up. So for extreme imbalance where you care about the positive class (fraud, rare disease, retrieval), use PR-AUC; for balanced data where overall ranking matters, use ROC-AUC.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why look at PR under imbalance?\" → a flood of negatives keeps FPR tiny, making ROC look good.\n- When reporting PR-AUC, state the baseline (positive rate).\n- Be able to implement AUC with the rank formula.","L0-0.3.2":"When an LLM is used as a classifier, it outputs a text label, so you can't directly draw a PR curve or tune a threshold. The key is turning the output into a thresholdable score and then choosing the operating point by business cost.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Use the logprob of each label's first token (or have the model output a confidence) as the score.\n2. Plot the PR curve on the validation set; under class imbalance, look at PR-AUC rather than overall accuracy.\n3. Choose the threshold by business cost: content moderation and risk review lean toward recall (backed by human review); scenarios that trigger automatic actions (deletion, money transfer) lean toward precision.\n4. For multiclass, state whether you use micro or macro averaging; for retrieval, use precision@k / recall@k.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLooking only at the label the model emits amounts to fixing an unknown threshold, with no way to trade precision against recall. LLM scores also need calibration: check with a reliability diagram and apply temperature scaling or Platt calibration if needed; verbally self-reported confidence is usually less stable than token probabilities, but also needs calibration. For narrow tasks with high QPS and low latency, a fine-tuned BERT-style encoder with a classification head is often cheaper.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Give the three steps: \"logprob as score → PR curve → threshold by cost\".\n- Explain the micro / macro choice.\n- Follow-up \"can you trust an LLM's confidence?\" → it needs calibration.","L0-0.3.3":"AUC only measures ranking, not whether probabilities are accurate. When probabilities feed directly into decisions (expected loss = p × amount), thresholds or multi-model blending, you must calibrate them.\n\n**How it works**\n- Example: `p³` and `p` have exactly the same AUC but very different ECE.\n- Methods: Platt scaling (fit a logistic regression on the logit); isotonic regression (non-parametric, for when data is plentiful); temperature scaling (for deep nets: fit a single scalar T to minimize validation NLL; it doesn't change the argmax, so accuracy is unaffected).\n- Evaluation: reliability diagram, Brier score, ECE.\n- Note: after training on downsampled majority-class data, probabilities must be corrected for the prior ratio.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNeural networks tend to be overconfident; LR minimizes log loss, so its outputs are naturally fairly well calibrated. Calibration breaks under distribution drift and needs periodic re-checking. Isotonic is more flexible but overfits on small data; Platt / temperature have few parameters and are more stable.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why calibrate if AUC is already high?\" → AUC is invariant to any monotonic transform.\n- Why temperature scaling doesn't affect accuracy.\n- Correct probabilities after downsampling; re-check calibration after distribution drift.","L0-0.3.4":"With extreme class imbalance (fraud, rare failures), a model can predict the majority class every time and still score high accuracy. There is a recommended order of remedies, starting with the cheapest and safest.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Change the metric first: PR-AUC, Recall@fixed FPR, business cost — not accuracy.\n2. Use class weights or focal loss in training.\n3. Downsample the majority class: faster training, but remember to correct probabilities for the prior.\n4. Only then consider oversampling such as SMOTE: it often helps little on high-dimensional or categorical features, and must be done only within training folds, otherwise synthetic samples leak into validation.\n5. Finally, tune the threshold against the cost matrix — often the single biggest win.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nResampling changes the training distribution and distorts output probabilities; class weights and threshold tuning leave the data alone and are easier to explain. Often the problem is not the model but the wrong metric and threshold. Also use time-respecting splits to avoid leakage.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer in order rather than jumping straight to SMOTE.\n- Stress that SMOTE must stay within folds and that downsampling requires probability correction.\n- The threshold is an operational decision: agree with the business on a minimum recall and a tolerable false-positive volume.","L0-0.3.5":"Cross-validation uses multiple train / validation splits to estimate generalization error more reliably. The splitting scheme must match the structure of the data, or the estimate will be overly optimistic.\n\n**How it works**\n- k-fold: for i.i.d. data.\n- Stratified k-fold: keeps class proportions consistent across folds under imbalance.\n- GroupKFold: when one user / patient has multiple records, group by entity so the same entity never appears in both training and validation.\n- Time series: rolling or expanding windows (`TimeSeriesSplit`), only using the past to predict the future; if needed, leave a gap between training and validation (purge / embargo) so feature windows don't cross the split point and leak.\n- Nested CV: when tuning hyperparameters and estimating generalization error at the same time, the inner loop selects hyperparameters and the outer loop evaluates, avoiding the optimistic bias of \"tune to the best validation score, then report it\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRandomly splitting temporal data, or letting one entity span folds, is a common reason offline metrics look inflated and then fail in production. Nested CV results are more trustworthy but cost several times as much compute as plain CV.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Choose the split based on the data (imbalance, groups, time).\n- Explain what purge / embargo do.\n- \"Why is the CV score reported after tuning optimistic?\" → leads into nested CV.","L0-0.3.6":"Data leakage means training uses information that isn't actually available at prediction time, inflating offline metrics and collapsing in production. It's a high-frequency interview topic, and common cases fall into five types.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Target leakage: a feature is not yet available at prediction time, e.g. using \"was a refund issued\" to predict fraud.\n2. Time travel: randomly splitting temporal data; feature windows that cross the label timestamp.\n3. Preprocessing leakage: fitting a scaler, PCA, target encoding or SMOTE on the full dataset before splitting. The correct approach is to put them in a `Pipeline` and fit within each fold.\n4. Entity duplication: the same user, the same device or near-duplicate images spread across training and test sets.\n5. Label-definition leakage: labels are produced by a rules engine whose inputs are the features themselves, so the model just learns the rules.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLeakage throws no errors; it just makes metrics \"suspiciously good\". How to investigate: check when each feature becomes available, split by time and entity, look for single features that are abnormally strong, and compare offline vs. online metrics.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- List all five types with an example of each.\n- Preprocessing must be fit within folds — the most commonly overlooked type.\n- When metrics look too good, your first reaction should be to check for leakage.","L0-0.3.7":"With multiple annotators and noisy labels, you can't just take a majority vote and train. The approach is to diagnose the noise first, then aggregate labels and filter likely mislabels, and finally train in a noise-robust way.\n\n**How it works**\n- Diagnosis: inter-annotator agreement (Cohen's κ / Krippendorff's α), each annotator's accuracy on gold-standard (gold / honeypot) items, noise structure (random / class-conditional / annotator-specific) and noise rate.\n- Aggregation: majority voting assumes everyone is equally reliable and errors are symmetric; Dawid–Skene uses EM to jointly estimate each annotator's confusion matrix and the posterior over each item's true label, so reliable annotators automatically get more weight.\n- Filtering (Confident Learning): use out-of-fold predicted probabilities and per-class confidence thresholds to find items where the model confidently assigns another class that disagrees with the given label; send them for re-annotation first rather than deleting them, since hard examples look a lot like mislabels.\n- Training: retrain after cleaning, or use soft labels, noise-robust losses (GCE, symmetric cross-entropy) and early stopping.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn a synthetic experiment with 5 annotators of uneven quality, majority voting scored 0.849 — even below the best single annotator at 0.855 — while Dawid–Skene reached 0.898. Cleaning helps hugely for 1-NN, which memorizes noise (0.623 → 0.909), and less for logistic regression (0.908 → 0.918). Dawid–Skene needs enough items labeled by multiple annotators; otherwise add a smoothing prior or anchor with gold standards.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Diagnose before treating; spend the re-annotation budget on items with uncertain posteriors.\n- Explain when majority voting is good enough.\n- Distinguish \"mislabeled\" from \"hard example\".","L0-0.3.8":"Feature engineering turns raw data into signals the model can use; training-serving skew is when online feature computation differs from training, degrading the model after launch.\n\n**How it works**\n- Numeric features: log, binning, standardization (not needed for tree models).\n- Categorical features: choose one-hot, target encoding, hashing or embeddings based on cardinality. Target encoding must be out-of-fold and smoothed, otherwise each sample's own label leaks into its feature.\n- Time features: cyclical encoding, time since an event.\n- Aggregate features: statistics per entity over multiple time windows, e.g. transaction counts and amounts over the last 1h / 24h / 7d.\n- Consistency: use the same feature definitions online and offline — exactly the problem a feature store solves.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIf features are computed in batch SQL offline and by separate code in real time online, even slight differences in definition or timing cause skew that is very hard to track down. A feature store provides unified definitions, point-in-time-correct offline backfills and low-latency online reads, at the cost of infrastructure.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why target encoding must be out-of-fold.\n- Time windows for aggregate features must not cross the label timestamp.\n- When it's \"good offline, bad online\", investigate training-serving skew.","L0-0.4.1":"Backpropagation applies the chain rule backward over the computation graph to compute gradients; initialization and architecture design decide whether gradients propagate stably through deep networks.\n\n**How it works**\n- Backpropagation: reverse-mode autodiff yields gradients for all parameters in one backward pass, but forward activations must be cached, and that is the main source of training memory (the matching optimization is activation checkpointing, trading recomputation for memory).\n- Vanishing / exploding gradients: a deep network's gradient is a product of many Jacobians; sigmoid saturation or badly scaled weights cause exponential decay or growth.\n- Initialization: Xavier / Glorot `Var = 2/(fan_in+fan_out)`, suited to tanh; He / Kaiming `Var = 2/fan_in`, suited to ReLU. Both aim to keep the variance of activations and gradients stable across layers.\n- Remedies: ReLU-family activations, residual connections (an identity path lets gradients flow straight through), normalization (stabilizes activation distributions), gradient clipping (suppresses explosions).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOnly in combination do these designs make networks with hundreds of layers trainable. He initialization has the extra factor of 2 because ReLU zeroes out half of its inputs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Where does training memory mostly go?\" → cached activations, mitigated by checkpointing.\n- Explain which problem each remedy addresses.\n- Xavier vs He formulas and the activations they suit.","L0-0.4.2":"Hand-coding conv2d is a common ML coding question. Write a naive loop version first to make sure it's correct, then rewrite the convolution as a single matrix multiply with im2col — which is also a common way frameworks lower convolutions to GEMM.\n\n**How it works**\n- Input `[N,C,H,W]`, kernel `[O,C,kh,kw]`, output size `floor((H+2p−k)/s)+1`.\n- Naive version: four nested loops over n, o, i, j; at each position, multiply the receptive field by the kernel elementwise and sum.\n- im2col version: flatten each receptive field into a row to get a `[N, Ho*Wo, C*kh*kw]` matrix, multiply by `w.reshape(O,−1).T`, and reshape back to `[N,O,Ho,Wo]`.\n- Backward: `dW = colsᵀ dY`, `dcols = dY Wᵀ`, then col2im folds dcols back into the input shape — identical to the backward pass of a matmul.\n- Note: PyTorch's conv2d actually computes cross-correlation and does not flip the kernel.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nim2col hands the computation to highly optimized GEMM and is fast, but the unrolled matrix duplicates elements, so its memory cost is roughly kh×kw times the original input. Measured, the maximum difference between the two implementations is on the order of 1e-15.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Get the output-size formula, padding and stride right.\n- Cross-check the im2col version against the naive one.\n- Be able to state the matrix form of the backward pass and the cross-correlation detail.","L0-0.4.3":"BatchNorm and LayerNorm both stabilize training through normalization; they differ in which dimension the statistics are computed over, and that determines where each applies.\n\n**How it works**\n- BatchNorm: normalizes each channel over the batch dimension; uses batch statistics in training and running averages at inference.\n- LayerNorm: normalizes each sample (token) independently over the feature dimension, `(x − μ)/√(σ²+ε) ⊙ γ + β`; independent of batch size, with identical behavior in training and inference.\n- RMSNorm: only divides by the root mean square and multiplies by γ, dropping mean-centering and the bias β, which cuts parameters from 2d to d; the assumption is that scale invariance is what matters. Both are invariant to overall scaling; only LN is invariant to overall shifts.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBN doesn't suit Transformers: sequence lengths vary and padding pollutes the statistics; large models have tiny per-GPU batches; and in autoregressive inference batch statistics don't match training. So Transformers use LN, and since Llama RMSNorm has been the default — cheaper to compute with comparable results. BN is still common in CNN vision tasks. In implementations, squaring overflows in fp16, so the computation is usually upcast to fp32.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why don't Transformers use BN?\" → the three reasons above.\n- How BN behaves differently in training vs inference.\n- What RMSNorm removes relative to LN and why that works.","L0-0.4.4":"CNNs process grid data such as images with local connectivity and weight sharing. The architecture itself provides translation equivariance, which makes CNNs data-efficient and cheap to compute.\n\n**How it works**\n- Local connectivity: each output looks only at a small window of the input.\n- Weight sharing: the same kernel slides across every position, so the parameter count is independent of image size.\n- Translation equivariance: shift the input and the feature map shifts accordingly; pooling adds some translation invariance.\n- The receptive field grows with depth, so deeper layers see larger regions and compose higher-level patterns.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCompared with Transformers, CNNs have strong inductive biases, do better on small data and have linear compute; Transformers connect any two positions in parallel through attention and scale better with data, at the cost of quadratic complexity in sequence length. Where CNNs are still the usual choice today: small image datasets, real-time or on-device vision, and backbones sensitive to latency and power. RNNs / LSTMs remain in niches such as low-power streaming sensors.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the difference between \"equivariant\" and \"invariant\".\n- Compare the inductive biases and complexity of CNN / RNN / Transformer.\n- \"When do we still use CNNs today?\" → small data, real-time on-device.","L0-0.4.5":"ResNet solved the difficulty of training deep networks with residual connections; ViT splits an image into patches and feeds them as tokens into a Transformer, forming the basis of modern vision and multimodal models.\n\n**How it works**\n- ResNet: `y = F(x) + x`; the identity mapping lets gradients flow straight through, so hundreds of layers can be trained. In essence the network learns a residual, so it's easy for a layer to \"do nothing\".\n- ViT: split the image into fixed-size patches (e.g. 16×16), so a 224×224 image yields 14×14 = 196 patches; flatten each patch and pass it through a linear layer (equivalent to a convolution with stride equal to the patch size) to get a token; add positional encodings and an optional `[CLS]` token and feed into a bidirectional Transformer encoder.\n- Cost formula: image tokens ≈ (H/patch) × (W/patch); doubling the resolution quadruples the token count.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nViT has almost no image-specific inductive bias (CNNs have locality and translation equivariance), so it underperforms CNNs on small data and needs large datasets or strong pretraining (the DINO series, CLIP) to pull ahead. Transformers inherited the residual connection idea directly.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why residuals mitigate vanishing gradients.\n- How to count ViT tokens, and how resolution affects VLM cost.\n- \"CNN or ViT with little data?\" → CNN, or a pretrained ViT.","L0-0.4.6":"RNNs / LSTMs were the workhorses for sequences before Transformers, and seq2seq used an encoder-decoder for sequence transduction; their bottleneck directly gave rise to the attention mechanism.\n\n**How it works**\n- RNN: updates the hidden state step by step in sequence; gradients are multiplied across time and easily vanish or explode.\n- LSTM / GRU: gates (input, forget, output) plus a cell state let information carry across time with less decay, mitigating vanishing gradients.\n- seq2seq: the encoder compresses the input sequence into a single fixed-length vector, from which the decoder generates the output. That fixed-length vector is an information bottleneck that hurts long sentences; attention (Bahdanau 2014) lets the decoder take a weighted mix of all encoder positions at each step, which eventually evolved into the Transformer.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLSTMs compute strictly sequentially, are hard to parallelize and handle long-range dependencies poorly. Why Transformers replaced them: parallelism, direct long-range connections, scalability. LSTMs are still used for streaming, low-power on-device and some time-series tasks, because their per-step memory footprint is fixed.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why did Transformers replace LSTMs?\" → parallelism, long-range dependencies, scalability.\n- \"Where are LSTMs still used?\" → streaming, on-device, some time series.\n- Explain that attention was born to fix seq2seq's fixed-length bottleneck.","L0-0.4.7":"Object detection finds the location and class of objects in an image; segmentation produces pixel-level class or instance masks. They are the two most frequently asked task types in CV fundamentals.\n\n**How it works**\n- Two-stage detection: Faster R-CNN first proposes candidate boxes with an RPN, then classifies and regresses each RoI; high accuracy, slower.\n- One-stage detection: the YOLO series and RetinaNet (which introduced focal loss to handle extreme foreground/background imbalance) output boxes in a single forward pass; YOLO-style models are the go-to for real-time.\n- Transformer detection: DETR frames detection as set prediction, assigning predictions to ground truth with Hungarian matching, eliminating NMS and anchors.\n- Semantic segmentation: FCN, U-Net (encoder-decoder + skip connections, common in medical imaging), DeepLab (dilated convolutions).\n- Instance / panoptic segmentation: Mask R-CNN, Mask2Former, outputting one mask per instance.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe detection metric is mAP@IoU; segmentation metrics are mIoU and Dice. NMS and anchors bring hyperparameters and post-processing overhead; DETR removes them but converges more slowly. U-Net's skip connections pass fine spatial detail from shallow layers to the decoder, making up for what downsampling loses.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Representative models and trade-offs of the three detector families.\n- Why U-Net needs skip connections.\n- What mAP and mIoU mean.","L0-0.4.8":"CLIP, SAM 3 and DINO represent three lines of vision foundation models — image-text alignment, promptable segmentation and self-supervised dense features — and they are the basis for understanding multimodal RAG and vision agents.\n\n**How it works**\n- CLIP: contrastive learning on 400M image-text pairs; an image tower and a text tower encode separately, pushing up the similarity of the N matching pairs in a batch and pushing down the other N² − N pairs (symmetric InfoNCE). The result is a shared semantic space supporting zero-shot classification (write each class as \"a photo of a {label}\" and pick the most similar) and text-to-image search. SigLIP switches to a pairwise sigmoid loss that doesn't depend on huge batches, and is one of the most common vision encoder families in VLMs.\n- SAM 3 (2025-11): prompted with text or exemplar images, it detects, segments and tracks all instances of a concept.\n- DINOv2 / DINOv3: self-supervised vision models that provide high-quality dense features; DINOv3 was trained on 1.7B unlabeled images and introduced Gram anchoring to fix dense-feature degradation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCLIP's training objective aligns whole images with whole sentences, so it's insensitive to fine-grained counting, spatial relations and small-text OCR, and its embeddings are unsuitable for pixel-level tasks; use DINO-style dense features for those.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain CLIP's symmetric InfoNCE and its zero-shot classification method.\n- CLIP's limitations and how they explain early VLM errors.\n- Choose DINO over CLIP for pixel-level tasks.","L0-0.4.9":"The PyTorch training loop is a must-know in DL engineering interviews: you should be able to explain what every line does and how Dataset and DataLoader divide the work.\n\n**How it works**\n- Training: after `model.train()`, for each batch run `optimizer.zero_grad()` → forward pass to compute the loss → `loss.backward()` → `optimizer.step()`.\n- `zero_grad`: clears gradients, because PyTorch accumulates gradients into `.grad` by default.\n- `backward`: traverses the autograd graph built during the forward pass in reverse, filling each parameter's `.grad`.\n- `step`: updates parameters from those gradients according to the optimizer's rule.\n- Evaluation: `model.eval()` switches dropout and BatchNorm to inference behavior; `torch.no_grad()` (or `inference_mode()`) doesn't record the graph, saving memory.\n- A Dataset defines how to fetch a single sample by index; a DataLoader handles batching, shuffling and parallel loading with multiple workers. A custom `collate_fn` is only needed for variable-length samples (e.g. padding text sequences, building attention masks).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe accumulation behavior is exactly what enables gradient accumulation: call step and zero_grad only every N steps, dividing the loss by N. `no_grad` and `eval()` are different things — the former governs the graph, the latter layer behavior — and you need both.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"What happens if you forget zero_grad?\" → gradients accumulate across batches.\n- The difference between `no_grad` and `eval()`.\n- Data-loading bottlenecks: more num_workers, pin_memory, prefetching, caching preprocessed data.","L0-0.5.1":"GANs learn a data distribution through an adversarial game between a generator G and a discriminator D. They can generate sharp samples in a single forward pass, but training is notoriously unstable.\n\n**How it works**\n- Objective: `min_G max_D E_x[log D(x)] + E_z[log(1 − D(G(z)))]` (Goodfellow et al., 2014).\n- With G fixed, the optimal discriminator is `D* = p_data/(p_data + p_g)`; substituting back, G's objective equals `2·JS(p_data ‖ p_g) − log 4`.\n- In practice G instead maximizes `log D(G(z))` (non-saturating loss), because early in training D easily spots fake samples and the original `log(1−D)` gradient is near 0.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThree root causes of instability:\n1. A game, not an optimization: alternating gradient descent seeks a saddle point and can oscillate without converging.\n2. Non-overlapping supports: real and fake distributions both lie on low-dimensional manifolds in high-dimensional space and barely intersect, so JS is stuck at log 2 and gradients vanish.\n3. Mode collapse: G finds a few modes that fool D and generates them repeatedly, collapsing diversity. Diagnose by checking whether samples from a batch of z are highly similar, whether FID's recall component is low, and whether a conditional GAN fails to cover all classes.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Derive the optimal D and state the JS divergence result.\n- The motivation for the non-saturating loss.\n- The three root causes of instability and how to diagnose mode collapse.","L0-0.5.2":"WGAN replaces JS divergence with the Wasserstein distance, fixing the vanishing gradients of the original GAN when supports don't overlap; WGAN-GP enforces the Lipschitz constraint better with a gradient penalty.\n\n**How it works**\n- The Wasserstein-1 (earth mover's) distance, via Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality, is `W = sup_{‖f‖_L ≤ 1} E_real[f] − E_fake[f]`.\n- The discriminator becomes a critic without a sigmoid and must be 1-Lipschitz.\n- The original WGAN enforces this with weight clipping; WGAN-GP uses a gradient penalty `λ·E[(‖∇_x̂ f(x̂)‖₂ − 1)²]`, where x̂ is a random interpolation on the line between real and fake samples and λ is typically 10.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe W distance stays continuous and has gradients even when supports don't overlap; the critic's loss correlates with sample quality and can be used to monitor training, which the original GAN can't do. Weight clipping limits the critic's expressiveness and is sensitive to the clipping threshold; the gradient penalty is more stable but requires an extra gradient w.r.t. the input each step. Other stabilization techniques: spectral normalization, TTUR (different learning rates for D and G), PacGAN (the discriminator sees several samples at once to suppress mode collapse), minibatch discrimination.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why W distance beats JS: it still has gradients when supports don't overlap.\n- Why the critic must be 1-Lipschitz; clipping vs gradient penalty.\n- Be able to list other stabilization tricks.","L0-0.5.3":"A conditional GAN adds conditioning information during generation so the model learns the conditional distribution p(x|y), letting you control what gets generated rather than just sampling at random.\n\n**How it works**\n- Feed the condition y (a class, text, another image) into both the generator G and the discriminator D.\n- G generates a sample from noise z and condition y; D judges whether the sample is real and matches the condition.\n- Representative work: pix2pix (image-to-image translation, conditioned on an input image); AC-GAN (the discriminator also outputs a class prediction, strengthening the conditioning with an auxiliary classification loss).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nConditioning makes generation controllable and underlies methods such as tabular synthesis (e.g. CTGAN's conditional vector). Conditional GANs still inherit GAN instability; during evaluation, check that every conditioning class is covered — incomplete class coverage is a sign of mode collapse. In images, mainstream controllable generation has moved to diffusion + classifier-free guidance / ControlNet.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The condition must go into both G and D, otherwise D can't penalize samples that are \"real but don't match the condition\".\n- The difference between pix2pix and AC-GAN.\n- How to diagnose mode collapse in a conditional GAN.","L0-0.5.4":"CTGAN (Xu et al., NeurIPS 2019) is a GAN designed specifically for tabular data, modified to handle two hard problems: multimodal continuous columns and imbalanced discrete columns. TVAE, proposed in the same paper, is the VAE counterpart.\n\n**How it works**\n- Mode-specific normalization: continuous columns are often multimodal and non-Gaussian (e.g. transaction amounts). First fit a variational Gaussian mixture (VGM) to find the modes, then represent each value as \"a one-hot of which mode it belongs to + a scalar normalized within that mode\".\n- Conditional vector + training-by-sampling: with severely imbalanced discrete columns, rare categories never get learned. During training, first pick a discrete column uniformly, then pick one of its values by log frequency as the condition, forcing G to cover minority categories.\n- Stability: WGAN-GP loss + PacGAN.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTVAE matches or beats CTGAN on many datasets, so run both as baselines. The common implementation is the SDV library (offering GaussianCopula, CTGAN, TVAE); note that it is licensed under the Business Source License 1.1, not an OSI open-source license, which restricts wrapping it as an external synthetic-data service — read the terms before commercial use.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The two hard problems and the two corresponding techniques.\n- \"Why also run TVAE and GaussianCopula?\" → no method dominates universally on tabular data.\n- SDV's licensing risk.","L0-0.5.5":"Evaluating synthetic data means answering three questions at once: does it look real (fidelity), is it useful (utility), and does it leak privacy (privacy)? It is a must-ask question in interviews for finance and similar domains.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Fidelity: per-column distributions (KS statistic, TVD), pairwise correlations / contingency tables, and whether business constraints hold (e.g. \"end date > start date\").\n2. Utility: TSTR — train on synthetic, test on real — compared against TRTR (train on real, test on real).\n3. Privacy: DCR (each synthetic record's distance to the closest real record, which should not be markedly smaller than distances within the real data), membership inference attack success rate, and re-identification risk from rare combinations.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nKey takeaway: synthetic data is not inherently privacy-preserving; generative models can memorize outlier records. When provable protection is required, use a DP-trained generator (CTGAN trained with DP-SGD, PATE-GAN, etc.), at the cost of lower fidelity. Privacy comes from the training mechanism, not the model type. The three dimensions are in tension: the higher the fidelity, the more likely the data hugs real records.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer along the three dimensions and name TSTR and DCR.\n- \"Is synthetic data safe by default?\" → no, it needs DP training.\n- Point out the fidelity vs privacy trade-off.","L0-0.5.6":"A VAE (variational autoencoder) is a generative model with an explicit latent space, trained by maximizing a lower bound on the log-likelihood (the ELBO). It is stable and supports interpolation and anomaly scoring.\n\n**How it works**\n- ELBO: `log p(x) ≥ E_{q(z|x)}[log p(x|z)] − KL(q(z|x) ‖ p(z))`. The first term is reconstruction; the second pulls the encoder's posterior toward the prior (usually a standard normal).\n- Reparameterization: `z = μ + σ ⊙ ε, ε ~ N(0, I)` moves the randomness into ε, which doesn't depend on the parameters, so sampling becomes differentiable and gradients flow back to the encoder.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPros: stable training (a single objective, no adversary), an explicit latent space, interpolation, and anomaly scoring (via the likelihood bound). Cons: samples tend to be blurry, because a Gaussian likelihood amounts to L2 reconstruction, which averages over multiple plausible outputs. Compared with GANs, VAEs cover modes more completely but are less sharp. In Stable Diffusion, the VAE only compresses images into a latent space, and diffusion happens in that latent space.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the ELBO and explain both terms.\n- Why reparameterization is necessary.\n- \"Why are VAEs blurry?\" → Gaussian likelihood ≈ L2 reconstruction.","L0-0.5.7":"Diffusion (DDPM, Ho et al., 2020) generates data by learning to denoise step by step. It trains stably and covers modes well, and has become the mainstream approach for high-quality image, audio and video generation.\n\n**How it works**\n- Forward process: add noise step by step, `q(x_t | x_0) = N(√ᾱ_t x_0, (1−ᾱ_t) I)`, so any t can be sampled from x_0 in one step.\n- Training: the network predicts the added noise, with loss `E‖ε − ε_θ(√ᾱ_t x_0 + √(1−ᾱ_t) ε, t)‖²`.\n- Sampling: start from pure noise and denoise step by step.\n- Acceleration: DDIM (deterministic sampling, fewer steps), step distillation (consistency models, etc.).\n- Conditional control: classifier-free guidance (CFG), extrapolating from a combination of conditional and unconditional predictions.\n- Cost reduction: latent diffusion runs diffusion in a VAE's latent space.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCompared with GANs, diffusion trains stably, has high diversity and strong controllability (CFG, ControlNet); the price is multi-step sampling — even after distillation it takes a few steps, so it's unsuitable for real-time single-step generation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the forward formula and the noise-prediction loss.\n- How CFG works and what it does.\n- Why latent diffusion cuts cost and why DDIM is faster.","L0-0.5.8":"Flow matching / rectified flow (Lipman et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2022) trains generative models on straight-line paths between noise and data. The objective is simpler than diffusion and sampling needs fewer steps.\n\n**How it works**\n- Interpolate linearly between noise x₀ and data x₁: `x_t = (1−t)x₀ + t·x₁`.\n- The network regresses the velocity field along that path: target `v = x₁ − x₀`, with MSE between predicted and target velocity as the loss.\n- To sample, start from noise and integrate an ODE to the data.\n- One training step: sample data x1, noise x0 and a random t, build xt, `loss = mse(model(xt, t), x1 − x0)`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nStraighter paths mean the ODE solver needs fewer steps; the training objective is plain regression, with no complicated noise-schedule derivations. Stable Diffusion 3 (Esser et al., 2024) adopted rectified flow. Mainstream image generation has moved from GANs to diffusion / flow matching. Note that papers differ on the direction of t (whether t=0 is noise or data); in an interview, state your convention first.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the interpolation formula and the velocity-field target.\n- \"Why fewer steps than DDPM?\" → straight paths.\n- Proactively state your convention for the direction of t.","L0-0.5.9":"Each generative model has scenarios where it shines. Choose based on real-time needs, quality, data type and privacy requirements rather than chasing the newest method.\n\n**How it works**\n- Real-time, single-step generation (on-device super-resolution, style transfer) → GAN: one forward pass; diffusion needs several steps even after distillation.\n- High-quality, high-diversity images / audio / video → diffusion / flow matching: stable training, full mode coverage, strong controllability (CFG, ControlNet).\n- Small-to-medium tabular synthetic data → first run GaussianCopula / CTGAN / TVAE baselines, then try tabular diffusion (e.g. TabDDPM) or LLM approaches; no method dominates on tabular data, so let TSTR and privacy metrics decide.\n- Text, logs, records with semantic constraints, few-shot settings → LLMs (prompted or fine-tuned generation): strong semantic consistency, but high cost, hard to control numeric distributions precisely, and may reproduce training data.\n- Need a latent representation or anomaly scoring → VAE (explicit likelihood lower bound).\n- Need provable privacy → any generator trained with DP.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhat's being tested is judgment: mapping latency, quality, controllability, cost and privacy to specific methods, and saying how you'd evaluate them.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Give a first choice and rationale for each requirement.\n- For tabular synthesis, stress \"baselines first, compare with TSTR\".\n- Privacy comes from the training mechanism, not the model type.","L0-0.6.1":"Industrial recommender systems generally use a multi-stage funnel: progressively heavier models narrow a huge item pool down to the dozen or so items finally shown, balancing quality against latency and cost.\n\n**How it works**\n- Full catalog: 10⁶–10⁹ items.\n- Retrieval / candidate generation: multiple retrieval channels (two-tower ANN, collaborative filtering, trending, rules), narrowing to ~10³.\n- Pre-ranking: a lightweight model, narrowing to ~10².\n- Ranking: GBDT / DeepFM / DCN / multi-task DNNs with rich cross features, narrowing to ~10¹.\n- Re-ranking: diversity (MMR), business rules, deduplication, ad blending.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nModel complexity at each stage is inversely proportional to the number of candidates: retrieval must cover a huge catalog in milliseconds and can only use precomputable vector search; ranking has few candidates, so it can afford heavy models and cross features. Each stage has a latency budget (e.g. on the order of 20–30 ms for retrieval and 30–50 ms for ranking). Items missed by upstream retrieval can't be recovered downstream no matter how strong the ranker, so retrieval emphasizes coverage and complementary channels.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Draw the four-stage funnel with each stage's scale and typical models.\n- Explain why you can't run the ranking model over the full catalog.\n- What re-ranking does: diversity, rules, blending.","L0-0.6.2":"Collaborative filtering recommends based on co-occurrence patterns in user–item interactions; matrix factorization is its most classic model-based form, representing users and items with low-dimensional vectors.\n\n**How it works**\n- Neighborhood CF: user-based / item-based recommendations from co-occurrence similarity; item-based is more stable and can be precomputed offline.\n- Matrix factorization: `r̂_ui = μ + b_u + b_i + p_uᵀq_i`, where μ is the global mean, b are user / item biases and p, q are latent vectors; optimize with SGD or ALS only on observed entries (with L2 regularization).\n- Verifying an implementation: the biased SGD version should beat both the global-mean and bias-only baselines on held-out RMSE; top-k recommendations must mask already-seen items.\n- Implicit feedback (clicks, plays): there are no negatives and unobserved does not mean disliked; common choices are BPR (pairwise ranking loss) or confidence-weighted ALS (WALS).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMatrix factorization is simple and scalable, and is the precursor to deep retrieval models such as two-tower; but it can't handle new users / items without interactions (cold start) and struggles to use content features.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the biased prediction formula and note that optimization is only over observed entries.\n- The difference between explicit ratings and implicit feedback, and the matching losses.\n- Why bias terms matter: they absorb users' rating habits and item popularity.","L0-0.6.3":"The two-tower model is the workhorse of industrial retrieval: a user tower and an item tower encode separately and the score is a vector dot product, so an ANN index can retrieve from millions of items in milliseconds.\n\n**How it works**\n- The two towers encode independently; item vectors are computed offline and indexed with ANN (HNSW / IVF-PQ), and online only the user vector is computed before searching.\n- Training uses in-batch softmax: items from other samples in the batch serve as negatives, the same structure as CLIP and DPR.\n- logQ correction: popular items appear as in-batch negatives more often and get over-penalized; correct this by subtracting the item's log sampling probability from the logit (Yi et al., RecSys 2019).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTwo-tower models are precomputable and scalable; the price is no early feature interaction between towers, which limits expressiveness, so they suit retrieval but not ranking. Negative quality drives results: in-batch negatives are free but skewed toward popular items, so mix in a few hard negatives and filter out false negatives.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why two-tower retrieval takes milliseconds: offline item vectors + ANN.\n- Why logQ correction is needed and how to do it.\n- \"Can two-tower do ranking?\" → no, it lacks feature crossing.","L0-0.6.4":"The ranking stage uses feature-rich models to precisely predict targets such as clicks and conversions, while dealing with offline metrics that don't match online gains, position bias and feedback loops.\n\n**How it works**\n- Features: mainly ID embeddings and cross features.\n- Models: Wide & Deep, DeepFM and DCN-v2 learn feature crosses automatically; multi-task architectures such as MMoE / PLE predict clicks, conversions and dwell time together, using multiple experts with task gates to reduce task conflict.\n- Offline metrics: AUC, GAUC (AUC grouped by user, closer to \"rank for each person\"), NDCG@K, Recall@K; A/B tests are the final word.\n- Bias: users can only click items that were shown, creating a feedback loop; items in top positions naturally get more clicks. Mitigate with IPS weighting, or add position as a training feature and set it to zero at prediction time.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOffline AUC gains don't necessarily translate into online gains, because AUC mixes comparisons across different users while recommendation only ranks within a single user, which is why GAUC is closer to reality. Ignoring bias makes the model keep reinforcing its own past choices.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- List representative models and the problems they solve (feature crossing, multi-task).\n- The difference between AUC and GAUC.\n- Two ways to mitigate position bias.","L0-0.6.5":"Cold start is about recommending when new users, new items or a new system lack interaction data and collaborative signals don't work. It's a high-frequency recommender-systems interview question.\n\n**How it works**\n- New users: popularity / region / demographic profiles, an interest survey at sign-up, real-time in-session signals, bandit exploration; after the first few interactions, an LLM can summarize what was engaged with into an interest description as an initial profile.\n- New items: map content features (text / image embeddings, generated by an LLM or CLIP) into the ID space, or initialize from similar items' embeddings; pair with an exploration traffic quota (a cold-start pool that allocates exposure via bandits or guaranteed-impression policies, quickly retiring or scaling items based on early clicks / completion rates).\n- New system: start with rules + content-based recommendation, iterating as interaction data accumulates.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nNew items that get no exposure never accumulate interaction data — a vicious cycle that also harms the platform ecosystem. Exploration traffic costs some short-term efficiency, so agree on a quota with the business. Report cold-start metrics (share of exposure for new items, new-item CTR, new-user next-day retention) separately rather than letting them drown in the overall numbers.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer in three parts: new users, new items, new system.\n- The idea of mapping content embeddings into the ID space.\n- The exploration-exploitation trade-off; evaluate cold-start slices separately.","L0-0.6.6":"LLMs enter recommender systems in four main ways, from easy-to-ship feature enhancement to the most radical, generative recommendation, which turns recommendation into \"autoregressively generate the next item ID\".\n\n**How it works**\n1. Features / encoder: encode item text and reviews into embeddings to ease cold start — the easiest to ship.\n2. Re-ranker / explanation generation: listwise re-ranking of the top-20 candidates plus generated recommendation reasons; constrained by latency and cost, so only used on small traffic slices or high-value scenarios.\n3. Generative recommendation: quantize items into Semantic IDs (tuples of discrete codes) with something like RQ-VAE, and have a sequence model autoregressively generate the next item's ID. Representative work is Meta's HSTU (ICML 2024) and Kuaishou's OneRec; the OneRec technical report says it has launched on Kuaishou / Kuaishou Lite, serving about 25% of QPS and replacing the multi-stage cascade with an end-to-end model.\n4. Conversational recommendation / agents: an LLM understands natural-language requests and calls the retrieval service as a tool.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFoundation models are moving into classical ML territory, and generative recommendation is going from papers to industry; but in production the multi-stage funnel with GBDT / DNNs is still the mainstay, and generative approaches face issues such as decoding latency and ID collisions.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- List the four uses in order of deployment difficulty.\n- Explain how Semantic IDs are built and their advantage (semantically similar items share prefixes).\n- Cite the two industrial examples, HSTU and OneRec.","L0-0.6.7":"Transformers are used in recommendation in two main ways: treating the behavior sequence as a sentence for sequential recommendation, and using target attention in ranking to produce a user-interest vector specific to each candidate.\n\n**How it works**\n- Sequential recommendation: SASRec uses causal self-attention to predict the next item from history (like GPT); BERT4Rec uses bidirectional attention + Cloze (mask items and predict them, like BERT).\n- Target attention (the DIN idea): Q = the embedding of the candidate item (plus context), K / V = the user's historical behaviors; attention weights express how relevant each piece of history is to the current candidate, and the output is a user-interest vector for that candidate, fed into the ranking network. DIN pointed out that a fixed-length user vector is a bottleneck that struggles to represent multi-peaked interests.\n- A template for defining QKV: Q comes from the thing being scored, K and V from the evidence being retrieved; mask out padding, and in training also mask future behaviors to avoid leakage.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDifferences from language models: a huge item vocabulary (millions of IDs), so sampled softmax / in-batch negatives are common; heterogeneous features (behavior type, timestamps); non-stationary data; millisecond latency budgets. Online, the user sequence's K and V are computed once and candidates are scored in parallel, at a cost of O(candidates × history length × d).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State clearly what Q, K and V are in recommendation cross-attention.\n- The difference between target attention and self-attention.\n- Leakage prevention: causal masks, time-based splits.","L0-0.6.8":"When designing an LLM-enhanced recommender, say up front: almost every company already has a mature recommendation funnel, and LLMs augment it rather than replace it.\n\n**How it works**\n- Keep the retrieval → pre-ranking → ranking → re-ranking funnel. Put LLMs on offline item understanding (tags, summaries, knowledge) and nearline user profiling (low-frequency batch summaries of behavior); the resulting vectors and tags enter existing models as features.\n- A back-of-the-envelope estimate explains why: if ranking sees ~500 candidates per request and each feeds 200 tokens to an LLM, one request is 100k tokens; at 12k QPS (assumed) that's 1.2B tokens per second — even at $0.10 per million tokens, about $10M per day, with latency far beyond 50 ms.\n- So LLMs can only sit offline / nearline, or touch very few candidates on very little traffic (e.g. re-ranking in a low-QPS shopping-assistant scenario).\n- Offline item understanding is cheap: about $4,500 one-off for 100M items (assumed pricing).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis design is low-risk, its gains usually show up first in cold start, and it can be iterated incrementally. Huawei's KAR is the canonical form of \"move LLM features offline\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use the cost estimate to argue why you can't call an LLM for ranking on every request.\n- Give a phased rollout (v1 offline features + semantic retrieval → sequence models → generative retrieval → small-traffic LLM re-ranking).\n- Gate every step with A/B tests plus guardrail metrics.","L0-0.6.9":"LLM-generated features are valuable, but a recommender's end-to-end latency budget is usually only a few hundred milliseconds (e.g. p99 < 200 ms), so generate features in tiers by how fast the signal changes, and have the online path only read from the feature store.\n\n**How it works**\n- Item side: static and bounded in number, so generate everything offline in batch, filling in new items asynchronously as they're ingested.\n- User side: nearline updates, refreshing the profile asynchronously after a session ends, with hour-level freshness; to control cost, only refresh active users whose behavior changed a lot, at low frequency.\n- Online: read only from the feature store. Call an LLM online only when the key signal exists only at request time (the current search query, conversation context) and the scenario's QPS is low, with a hard timeout (e.g. 300 ms, assumed), falling back to the ranker's order on timeout.\n- Feature versioning: online must read the same version of LLM features that training used; release and roll back the model and feature versions together.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe cost of offline features is freshness; the cost of online calls is latency and money. If the offline LLM features switch model or prompt while the ranker was trained on the old ones, you get training-serving skew — a problem that throws no errors but quietly degrades results.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The three tiers: items offline, users nearline, online read-only.\n- The preconditions for calling an LLM online, and the timeout fallback.\n- What feature version numbers are for.","L0-0.6.10":"Sequential recommendation treats a user's behavior sequence as a \"sentence\" and items as \"tokens\", borrowing language-model architectures to predict what the user will interact with next. It's mostly used for retrieval and user representations.\n\n**How it works**\n- SASRec (ICDM 2018): causal self-attention predicting the next item from history — the GPT of recommendation.\n- BERT4Rec (CIKM 2019): a bidirectional encoder doing Cloze-style masked item prediction (randomly mask items in the sequence and predict them) — the BERT of recommendation.\n- Besides the item ID, input embeddings usually add position, time gap and behavior type (click / purchase / full view).\n- The output user vector goes straight into u2i ANN retrieval.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCompared with average-pooling history into one vector, sequence models capture behavior order and recent interest shifts. Unlike language models, the item vocabulary can reach millions or even hundreds of millions, making full softmax infeasible, so use sampled softmax or in-batch negatives; in training, prevent leakage of future behavior (causal masks, time-based splits). Bidirectional BERT4Rec gets a richer training signal, but at inference it needs a mask position appended to the end of the sequence to predict the next item.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- SASRec and BERT4Rec correspond to GPT and BERT.\n- What gets added to the input besides the ID.\n- Training tricks for large vocabularies and leakage prevention.","L0-0.6.11":"Target attention is cross-attention in recommendation ranking: the candidate item \"queries\" the user's historical behaviors to produce a user-interest vector specific to that candidate.\n\n**How it works**\n- Q = the embedding of the candidate (target) item, optionally concatenated with context features; K = V = the embeddings of each historical item in the user's behavior sequence (plus position, time gap, behavior type).\n- The weight `α_i = softmax(q·k_i / √d)` is the relevance of the i-th historical behavior to the current candidate; the output `Σ α_i v_i` is concatenated with user, item and context features and fed into an MLP to predict CTR / CVR.\n- Why the candidate is Q: the same user should attend to different history for different candidates — when scoring basketball shoes, sports-related history is amplified and baby-product history suppressed.\n- Lineage: Alibaba's DIN (KDD 2018) was the first to weight historical behaviors by the candidate; BST used a Transformer to model \"behavior sequence + target item\" and was deployed on Taobao.\n- Engineering: the hundreds of candidates in one request share the same user sequence, so the K / V projections are computed once and candidate Qs are batched — the same idea as reusing the KV cache in LLMs; when the sequence is too long, first retrieve the few hundred most relevant entries, then apply attention.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOne-line distinction: self-attention models relations among historical behaviors and is mostly used for retrieval / user representations; target attention models the relation between candidate and history and is standard in ranking. Real systems often encode history with self-attention first, then apply target attention.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State precisely where Q, K and V come from.\n- Complexity is about candidates × sequence length × d, plus optimizations for long sequences.\n- The difference from self-attention.","L0-0.6.12":"Generative retrieval encodes items as semantic IDs and has a sequence model autoregressively generate the next item's ID. It's promising, but the engineering risks are real; the safe approach is to launch it first as one small-traffic channel among several retrieval channels.\n\n**How it works**\n- Represent items with content embeddings, then apply multi-level residual quantization to get a tuple of codewords (e.g. 3 levels) as the semantic ID; semantically similar items share prefixes, and the vocabulary shrinks from an embedding table with hundreds of millions of rows to a few thousand codeword tokens.\n- Train a Transformer to autoregressively generate the next item's ID from user history, decoding candidates online with beam search.\n- Representative work: Google's TIGER (semantic IDs via RQ-VAE), Meta's HSTU, Kuaishou's OneRec (the technical report says it serves about 25% of QPS).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Pros: end-to-end, good semantic generalization, cold-start friendly (a new item gets a meaningful ID as soon as it's ingested).\n- Cons: beam-search decoding latency; ID collisions (need a disambiguation position); the codebook drifts with the item catalog and must be retrained (map old to new IDs during retraining and dual-write for a period); a tendency toward popular items and diversity collapse.\n- Mitigations: keep the multi-channel retrieval as a safety net, add diversity constraints in re-ranking, and monitor coverage and long-tail exposure.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain how semantic IDs are built and their benefits.\n- Name at least three risks and their mitigations.\n- Rollout path: start as one small-traffic channel in an A/B test, and only discuss replacing the cascade after proving incremental gains.","L0-0.6.13":"A recommender whose offline metrics improve while the online A/B test stays flat or even drops is a very common interview follow-up. You should be able to list the causes systematically and say how to investigate.\n\n**How it works**\nCommon causes:\n1. Exposure bias: you can only evaluate on samples that were exposed; offline data is shaped by the old model, and items the new model prefers have no labels.\n2. Feature time travel: training used information only available after prediction time.\n3. Training-serving skew: online feature computation differs from training in definition or version (e.g. the LLM features changed version).\n4. Offline metrics misaligned with business goals: e.g. AUC rises while GAUC doesn't — AUC mixes comparisons across users, while recommendation only ranks within a single user.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRemedies: split evaluation sets by time; timestamp and version features; use metrics closer to online ranking such as GAUC and NDCG; keep a small slice of random traffic for unbiased evaluation; use position as a training feature and set it to zero at prediction time. For online A/B tests, split at the user level, run at least 1–2 weeks to rule out novelty effects, and set guardrail metrics (p99 latency, cost per request, negative-feedback rate).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Name all four causes.\n- Explain why AUC and GAUC can diverge.\n- Describe improvements to offline evaluation and norms for online experiments.","L0-0.6.14":"A multimodal embedding model maps items (cover images, video frames, titles, ASR / OCR text), queries and users into one vector space for semantic retrieval, i2i and cold start. Training it on limited compute (e.g. 8 GPUs and a two-week training window, assumed) requires careful trade-offs.\n\n**How it works**\n- Data: content pairs (image–title, video frame–ASR) are plentiful but noisy and need deduplication and similarity filtering; behavior pairs (query → click, co-click / co-purchase) carry collaborative signal and are closer to the business; remove accidental clicks, downsample popular items, and split evaluation sets by time.\n- Loss: CLIP-style symmetric InfoNCE (learnable τ); or SigLIP's sigmoid loss, which treats each pair as an independent binary classification with no global normalization and performs better with small batches.\n- Negatives: in-batch negatives are free; mix in a few hard negatives and filter false negatives; popular items get over-penalized, so apply logQ correction.\n- Large batches: GradCache (first run a no-graph forward pass to get the whole batch's embeddings and gradients, then recompute the backward pass in sub-batches, so memory is almost independent of batch size), all-gather across GPUs to share negatives, mixed precision + checkpointing, and freezing the backbone to train only a projection head or LoRA.\n- Recipe: start from an open-source CLIP / SigLIP, do domain adaptation on content pairs first, then fine-tune on behavior pairs; use Matryoshka to train multiple truncatable dimensions at once.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nContrastive learning depends on large batches and good negatives — exactly the pain point for compute-constrained teams; SigLIP and GradCache are ready-made solutions.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How content pairs and behavior pairs serve different purposes.\n- Hard negatives + logQ correction.\n- Ways to get large batches with little GPU memory.","L0-0.6.15":"When deploying a multimodal embedding model where no extra GPUs are allowed online, the core idea is to move all heavy computation offline, deploy only lightweight components online, and use compression to cut storage and latency.\n\n**How it works**\n- Everything item-side offline: image / video towers run only in offline batch jobs; online deploys only a small text or user tower.\n- Distillation: distill a large teacher (e.g. ViT-L class) into a small student, aligning embeddings with a cosine loss plus a loss that preserves the relative similarity ranking within the batch, so the student inherits retrieval ranking rather than just fitting coordinates.\n- Quantization: INT8 / FP8 weights for inference; int8 scalar quantization or PQ for vectors. Scale: 100M × 256 dims is about 51 GB in fp16, about 26 GB in int8, and about 6.4 GB with PQ at 64 bytes per vector (all assumed scales).\n- Dimensionality reduction: Matryoshka truncation to 128 or 256 dims.\n- Version switching: changing the model requires recomputing all item vectors and rebuilding the index; old and new vector spaces can't be queried together, so use dual indexes with traffic switching.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEvery compression step costs some recall, so gate them with offline Recall@K and downstream retrieval A/B tests. Choose the ANN index by scale: Flat / HNSW for millions; IVF-PQ for tens of millions to hundreds of millions under memory limits; DiskANN-style on-disk indexes beyond that.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why does switching models require a full recompute?\" → the vector spaces are incompatible.\n- Why distillation needs a ranking-preservation loss.\n- Be able to estimate vector storage and choose an index accordingly.","L0-0.7.1":"Time series forecasting has a \"method ladder\" from simple to complex. The core principle: each rung up must beat the one below to justify itself — don't start by piling on complex models.\n\n**How it works**\n- Baselines: naive (the last value) and seasonal naive (the value at the same position in the previous period).\n- Statistical models: ETS / ARIMA / Prophet, suited to single series and interpretable.\n- GBDT + lag / rolling-window / calendar features: the industrial workhorse for many-series settings; most top M5 competition solutions were based on LightGBM.\n- Deep models: DeepAR, N-BEATS, TFT, PatchTST.\n- Time series foundation models: TimesFM, Chronos, capable of zero-shot forecasting.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSeasonal naive is often hard to beat by much, and it's the denominator of the MASE metric. GBDT turns forecasting into tabular regression, using cross-series information and external features cheaply and is easy to deploy; deep and foundation models only pay off with many series, complex patterns or cold start, at the cost of compute and interpretability.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Start with the baseline, then explain why you'd move up — this shows engineering judgment.\n- Follow-up \"why does GBDT often beat deep models?\": data scale, feature engineering, tuning and deployment cost.\n- Lag features may only use data before the cutoff, otherwise they leak.","L0-0.7.2":"A rolling-origin backtest is the only correct way to validate time series models: at multiple time cutoffs, train only on data before the cutoff, forecast a window after it, then aggregate the errors across cutoffs.\n\n**How it works**\n- Choose cutoffs t1 < t2 < … in order, train once per cutoff (expanding or sliding window), and evaluate the next h steps.\n- Never split randomly: a random split lets the model \"see the future\", badly underestimating error.\n- Lag and rolling features must also be computed only from data before the cutoff.\n\n**Choosing metrics**\n- Use MAPE with care: it explodes when actual values approach 0 and penalizes under- and over-forecasting asymmetrically.\n- MASE: the error ratio relative to seasonal naive; below 1 means better than the baseline, and it's comparable across series.\n- WAPE: total absolute error divided by total actuals, robust to zeros and common in retail.\n- Pinball loss: for quantile / probabilistic forecasts.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBacktesting over multiple cutoffs shows how stable the model is across seasons and regimes, at the cost of multiplying the number of training runs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not use random K-fold? Answer: temporal dependence causes information leakage.\n- Be able to explain MAPE's two flaws.\n- For probabilistic forecasts, report pinball loss / coverage, not just point error.","L0-0.7.3":"Time series foundation models are large models pretrained on massive collections of series that can forecast unseen series zero-shot, offering a solution for cold start and quick baselines. Representative models are Google's TimesFM and Amazon's Chronos.\n\n**How it works**\nSlice the history window into patches or discrete tokens, feed them into a Transformer, and predict the next several steps (optionally as quantiles). Key versions:\n- TimesFM 2.5 (2025-09-15): 200M parameters, 16k context.\n- Chronos-2 (2025-10): zero-shot forecasting for univariate, multivariate and covariate-informed settings.\n- TimesFM-3 (2026-08-31): 330M parameters, native support for multivariate inputs, past covariates and known future covariates.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEarly versions were univariate only, so common business covariates such as promotions, prices and holidays couldn't be used; newer versions cover multivariate and covariate-informed forecasting, lowering the bar for real business use. But the production workhorse is still often GBDT: low cost, low latency, interpretable. A sensible positioning is to use foundation models for zero-shot baselines and cold start, and GBDT as the mainstay when data is plentiful and latency matters.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Zero-shot baseline vs a trained GBDT: how to compare them fairly with a rolling-origin backtest.\n- What past covariates and known future covariates are.\n- Inference cost and latency of foundation models.","L0-0.7.4":"Anomaly detection finds samples that deviate from normal patterns when there are no (or very few) labels. It's widely used in monitoring, risk control and quality inspection; pick the method by data shape and scale.\n\n**How it works**\n- Statistical rules: z-score, IQR, or STL decomposition followed by looking at residuals; suited to single-metric monitoring.\n- Isolation Forest: pick random features and random splits; anomalies are easier to isolate and have shorter average paths. Suited to tabular data and the go-to unsupervised baseline.\n- LOF / kNN distance: a point whose local density is markedly lower than its neighbors' is anomalous; suited to small and medium data.\n- Autoencoder / VAE: train only on normal data, and flag large reconstruction error as anomalous; suited to high-dimensional and sequential data.\n- One-class SVM: learns a boundary enclosing normal samples; suited to small data.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nUnsupervised methods tell you something is \"unusual\", not necessarily \"bad\"; thresholds need calibrating against business cost and human review. Distance-based methods break down in high dimensions and are compute-heavy; Isolation Forest is cheap and scale-insensitive; deep methods are expressive but you must guard against the model reconstructing anomalies well too. Once you have labels, move to supervised or semi-supervised learning.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why a short path in Isolation Forest signals an anomaly.\n- How to evaluate without labels: manual spot checks, injecting synthetic anomalies, measuring alert precision.\n- For time series anomalies, remove trend and seasonality first.","L0-0.7.5":"Fraud detection looks like binary classification, but several peculiarities make it very different from ordinary classification, and they're a focus in interviews at payments and finance companies.\n\n**Six things that set it apart**\n1. Extreme imbalance: fraud is often below 0.1% of transactions, so look at PR-AUC, recall at a fixed FPR and amount-weighted metrics, not accuracy or ROC-AUC.\n2. Delayed labels: chargebacks may only be confirmed weeks or months later, so training must cut off at a maturity window, and labels on recent samples can't be trusted.\n3. Adversarial drift: fraudsters adapt to the model, requiring continuous monitoring and periodic retraining.\n4. Features: mainly entity aggregates and velocity features (transactions on the same card in the last 1h, new device, geographic jumps); graph features over shared devices / IPs are very valuable.\n5. Real-time: authorization decisions usually must complete within tens of milliseconds, so it's online features + GBDT scoring, with a rules engine as a fallback and human review for the gray zone.\n6. Blocking changes the labels: you never learn the true label of a blocked transaction, so keep a small amount of random pass-through or use counterfactual evaluation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPoint 6 is classic selection bias: the better the model, the more biased the training data. Random pass-through costs a small amount of loss — the price of unbiased labels.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why the threshold is set from the cost matrix rather than F1.\n- How to handle evaluation and retraining cadence under delayed labels.","L0-0.8.1":"FedAvg (McMahan et al., 2017) is the most basic federated learning algorithm: data stays on each client, only model parameters are uploaded, and the server aggregates them, enabling joint training without centralizing data.\n\n**How it works**\nEach round:\n1. The server samples some clients and sends them the global model w_t.\n2. Each client runs E epochs of SGD on its local data to get w_t^k.\n3. The server takes a sample-count-weighted average: `w_{t+1} = Σ_k (n_k / n) · w_t^k`.\n\nWeighting by n_k is because the global objective is the average loss over all samples; equal weighting would over-represent small clients. When E = 1 and each client takes a single full-batch gradient step, gradients are additive over samples, so FedAvg is equivalent to one step of centralized GD, i.e. FedSGD; `code/ml/fedavg.py` verifies this with an assertion.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTaking several local steps drastically cuts the number of communication rounds — FedAvg's core advantage over FedSGD; the cost is client drift on non-IID data. Real systems must also handle clients dropping out, arriving late or being malicious.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the weighted formula and explain why it's weighted by sample count.\n- Derive the equivalence with FedSGD.\n- The trade-off in local steps: communication vs drift.","L0-0.8.2":"Non-IID means clients have different data distributions, which is the norm in federated learning. It causes client drift: the longer local training runs, the more each client drifts toward its own local optimum, and the averaged model moves away from the global optimum.\n\n**How it works**\nNon-IID includes label skew, feature skew, quantity skew and concept shift. In a code experiment (with different true weights per client), the distance from the centralized optimum was about 1e-14 with 1 local step, about 0.03 with 5 steps and about 0.31 with 50 steps — a clear illustration of drift growing with local steps.\n\nRemedies:\n- FedProx: add a proximal term `μ/2 ‖w − w_t‖²` to the local objective so local parameters can't stray too far from the global model.\n- SCAFFOLD: use control variates to estimate and correct each client's gradient bias.\n- Server-side optimizers: e.g. FedAdam, which treats the aggregated update as a pseudo-gradient.\n- Personalization: shared backbone + local head, clustered federated learning.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFewer local steps ease drift but increase communication rounds; personalization gives up \"one global model\" in exchange for better performance on each client.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why many local steps shift FedAvg's fixed point away from the global optimum.\n- FedProx vs SCAFFOLD: the former constrains distance, the latter corrects direction.\n- Experiments commonly use Dirichlet(α) to create label skew; smaller α means more skew.","L0-0.8.3":"\"Data never leaves the device\" does not mean privacy is guaranteed. A federated learning threat model must distinguish where attacks come from and defend against each with different tools.\n\n**Three threats and their defenses**\n- The server reconstructs data from an individual update: e.g. gradient inversion (Deep Leakage from Gradients) can rebuild training samples from gradients. Defense: secure aggregation, so the server only sees the sum.\n- Inferring individuals from the aggregate model: e.g. membership inference, determining whether a record was used in training. Defense: differential privacy (clipping + noise).\n- Malicious clients: data poisoning, implanted backdoors. Defense: robust aggregation (coordinate-wise median, trimmed mean, Krum), update-norm limits, anomaly detection.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese defenses complement rather than replace one another: SecAgg stops the server seeing individual updates but doesn't stop membership inference from the final model; DP protects individuals but costs accuracy; robust aggregation needs to see individual updates, which is in tension with SecAgg. Krum picks the update closest to the others, while median / trimmed mean drop extreme values per coordinate; under non-IID data they can wrongly penalize clients that are normal but unusual.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Organize the answer by \"who is the attacker and what are they attacking\".\n- Why SecAgg and robust aggregation are hard to use together.\n- SecAgg and DP can be combined into distributed DP.","L0-0.8.4":"Secure aggregation (Bonawitz et al., CCS 2017) is a cryptographic protocol that lets the server obtain only the sum of all clients' updates and never any individual client's update, defending against gradient inversion.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each pair of clients (i, j) derives a shared random mask m_ij via key agreement.\n- Client i uploads `x_i + m_ij` and client j uploads `x_j − m_ij` (for every pair).\n- When the server sums them, the paired masks cancel out, yielding `Σ x_i`, while each individual upload looks like random noise.\n- Secret sharing handles clients that drop out mid-round: the remaining clients help recover the dropped client's masks so they can be removed.\n\nThe toy version in `code/ml/fedavg.py` asserts that \"each masked vector differs greatly from the true update, but the sum is unchanged\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nFedAvg itself only needs a weighted sum, so SecAgg costs no accuracy; the price is communication and compute overhead and complex dropout handling. It doesn't prevent membership inference (the sum or final model may still leak), and it makes it hard for the server to check whether an individual update is malicious, which conflicts with robust aggregation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the \"paired masks cancel\" mechanism.\n- What about dropouts: secret sharing.\n- Division of labor between SecAgg and DP: the former hides individual updates, the latter bounds an individual's influence on the result.","L0-0.8.5":"DP-FedAvg adds differential privacy to federated averaging, bounding the influence of any single client on the aggregate and thereby defending against attacks such as membership inference that infer individuals from the model.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Clipping: clip each client's update to an L2 norm of at most C, bounding sensitivity.\n2. Noise: add Gaussian noise N(0, σ²C²I) to the aggregated (summed) result, then divide by the number of participating clients.\n3. Accounting: use an RDP / moments accountant to track the cumulative privacy budget (ε, δ) spent over training rounds.\n\n(This is a simplified description that omits client sampling rates and accountant details.)\n\n**Central DP vs local DP**\n- Central DP: a trusted server adds the noise, which is relatively small.\n- Local DP: each client adds noise before uploading, so the server need not be trusted, but far more noise is needed and accuracy suffers more.\n- SecAgg and DP can be combined (distributed DP): each client adds part of the noise, and SecAgg ensures the server sees only the sum.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSmaller ε means stronger privacy and a worse model; too small a C clips away useful signal, too large requires more noise. The more clients participate, the less the noise matters after averaging.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why you must clip first: without bounded sensitivity you can't calibrate the noise.\n- Explain what (ε, δ) means and how the privacy budget accumulates over rounds.\n- Why local DP requires more noise.","L0-0.8.6":"Federated learning splits into two categories by participant scale and reliability, cross-device and cross-silo, and their engineering problems are very different.\n\n**The two compared**\n- Cross-device: the classic example is next-word prediction on phone keyboards. Participants are millions of unreliable devices that drop out at any time and are limited in compute and battery; each round can only sample a small fraction. The focus is communication efficiency, dropout tolerance and on-device privacy.\n- Cross-silo: the classic example is across banks or hospitals. There are a few to a few dozen reliable participants that can join every round with ample compute; the concerns are compliance, auditing and incentives (how to share the gains according to each party's contribution).\n\n**Finance**\nMostly cross-silo, often combined with vertical federated learning and MPC. Vertical FL means each party holds different features for the same users (e.g. a bank has transactions, an e-commerce company has shopping behavior), requiring private set intersection to align samples before joint training; horizontal FL means the parties have the same features for different users, which is where FedAvg belongs.\n\n**Frameworks**\nFlower is actively developed and framework-agnostic; NVIDIA FLARE leans toward enterprise and healthcare.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Identify which category a scenario belongs to and name its main challenges.\n- Horizontal vs vertical federated learning.\n- With few participants in cross-silo, DP noise and robust aggregation designs change accordingly.","L0-0.8.7":"Edge AI squeezes models onto devices with limited compute, memory and power. The three common compression techniques are quantization, distillation and pruning.\n\n**How it works**\n- Quantization: PTQ uses a calibration set to measure activation ranges and quantizes weights and activations to int8 / int4; QAT inserts fake-quant ops during training and uses the straight-through estimator (STE) so gradients pass through rounding. Per-channel is more accurate than per-tensor.\n- Distillation: the student fits the teacher's soft labels, `L = α·CE(y, s) + (1−α)·T²·KL(softmax(t/T) ‖ softmax(s/T))`; the temperature T lets soft labels carry inter-class similarity, and T² balances gradient magnitudes.\n- Pruning: unstructured pruning zeroes individual weights and needs sparse kernels to actually speed up; structured pruning removes whole channels or attention heads and is directly faster.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- PTQ is cheap but loses noticeable accuracy on small models or at low bit widths; QAT is more accurate but needs a training pipeline.\n- Distillation incurs teacher inference costs, and student capacity sets the ceiling.\n- Pruned models usually need fine-tuning to recover accuracy.\nThe three are often combined, e.g. distill a small model first, then apply structured pruning, and finally quantize.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When to choose PTQ vs QAT.\n- Why unstructured pruning gives \"fewer parameters but no speedup\".\n- The role of T² in the distillation loss.","L0-0.8.8":"An on-device runtime is the software stack that runs inference for trained models on phones and embedded devices. As of 2026-10, both major ecosystems have converged on \"native export paths from the training framework\".\n\n**Main options**\n- PyTorch ecosystem: ExecuTorch, with 1.0 GA; the PyTorch 2.13 release blog says it has been merged into PyTorch Core; models are usually exported via `torch.export`.\n- Google ecosystem: LiteRT. TF 2.20 (2025-08) deprecated `tf.lite` in favor of the standalone LiteRT; TF 2.21 (2026-03) said LiteRT's new runtime is in production, with GPU performance about 1.4× TFLite, and supports conversion from PyTorch / JAX models.\n- Others: Core ML on Apple platforms, ONNX Runtime across platforms.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOn-device choices depend on more than accuracy: NPU support on the target hardware (whether ops can be dispatched to the accelerator), operator coverage (unsupported ops fall back to CPU, slowing things down or failing outright), package size and power. NPU support has become a key selection criterion.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked about \"TFLite\", point out that it has been renamed and migrated to LiteRT.\n- Explain the path from training to device: PyTorch → export → ExecuTorch.\n- How to assess operator coverage and fallback during selection.","L0-0.9.1":"Before launching an A/B test, estimate how many samples each group needs to detect an effect of the expected size with enough power, so the experiment neither ends inconclusively nor wastes traffic.\n\n**How it works**\nFor a two-sided test, the per-group sample size is `n ≈ 2(z_{1−α/2} + z_{1−β})² σ² / δ²`, where δ is the minimum effect to detect and σ² is the metric's variance; for conversion-rate metrics `σ² = p(1−p)`.\n\nExample: baseline conversion 5%, aiming to detect an absolute lift of 0.5 percentage points, α = 0.05, power = 0.8 (z sum ≈ 2.80), approximating both groups with the baseline variance: `n ≈ 2 × 2.80² × 0.0475 / 0.005² ≈ 29.8k` per group; using the summed variances of 5% and 5.5%, 0.1022, gives about 32k. In an interview, \"about 30k\" is fine.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nn is inversely proportional to δ²: halving the detectable effect quadruples the sample size. Small effects therefore need huge traffic, in which case variance reduction such as CUPED can shrink σ². Smaller α and higher power both increase the sample size.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to do the example above in your head.\n- Explain \"halve the effect, 4× the samples\".\n- What to do when samples are insufficient: run longer, use variance reduction, switch to a more sensitive metric.","L0-0.9.2":"Statistical tests for A/B experiments should be chosen by metric type, and you must first check that assignment itself is sound (SRM), otherwise the conclusions are invalid.\n\n**Choosing a test by scenario**\n- Two proportions: two-proportion z-test / chi-square test.\n- Two means: Welch's t-test (no equal-variance assumption); with large samples the central limit theorem suffices.\n- Heavy-tailed or skewed metrics (revenue, time spent): Mann–Whitney, bootstrap, or winsorize first.\n- Ratio metrics (e.g. clicks / impressions when the randomization unit is the user): use the delta method or bootstrap. A plain t-test underestimates variance because multiple impressions from the same user are not independent.\n\n**SRM (Sample Ratio Mismatch)**\nRun a chi-square test on the actual number of units assigned to each group against the designed ratio (e.g. 50/50); a very small p means there's a bug in the experiment pipeline (assignment, logging, filtering), and the results should be discarded rather than interpreted.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA mismatch between the analysis unit and the randomization unit is the most common source of errors; SRM checks cost almost nothing yet block a great many wrong conclusions.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why ratio metrics need the delta method.\n- How to investigate once you find SRM.\n- Why not to use a t-test directly on heavy-tailed metrics.","L0-0.9.3":"Peeking and multiple comparisons are the two most common sources of \"false significance\" in A/B testing; both push the actual Type I error rate far above the nominal α.\n\n**Peeking**\nRepeatedly checking results and stopping as soon as they look significant amounts to running many tests and stopping whenever one happens to be significant, which inflates the false-positive rate. Reference measurement: in an A/A normal simulation with 10 evenly spaced peeks and two-sided α = 0.05, the false-positive rate is about 19%. Remedies: fix the experiment duration, or use sequential testing (group sequential, mSPRT / always-valid p-values), which allows looking at any time while controlling the error rate.\n\n**Multiple comparisons**\nWhen you look at many metrics or segments at once, some will be significant by chance. Remedies: Bonferroni correction (divide α by the number of tests; strict, controls FWER) or Benjamini–Hochberg (controls FDR; more lenient, higher power), and pre-specify a single primary metric.\n\n**Related pitfalls**\nNovelty / primacy effects: run for at least one to two full weekly cycles. Statistical significance is not business significance — report confidence intervals, effect sizes and guardrail metrics.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why peeking increases false positives.\n- Bonferroni vs BH (FWER vs FDR).\n- How to design an A/A simulation to verify this.","L0-0.9.4":"CUPED is the most widely used variance reduction method in A/B testing: it uses pre-experiment data to explain away part of the metric's fluctuation, so the same power needs fewer samples and shorter experiments.\n\n**How it works**\nUse the same metric from before the experiment, X, as a covariate (e.g. a user's spend over a period before the experiment) and adjust the in-experiment metric Y:\n`Y' = Y − θ(X − X̄)`, where `θ = cov(X, Y) / var(X)`.\nBecause X is produced before the experiment and is independent of assignment, the adjustment doesn't change the expected treatment effect; it only reduces variance, by a factor of (1 − ρ²), where ρ is the correlation between X and Y. Spot check: at ρ = 0.7 the measured variance ratio is 0.513 vs a theoretical 0.51.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSince sample size is proportional to variance, ρ = 0.7 roughly halves the required sample size. The gain depends on how well the pre-experiment metric correlates with the in-experiment one: new users have no history, so the benefit is limited; the covariate must not be affected by the experiment, or it introduces bias. In essence it is equivalent to linear regression adjustment with a covariate.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the formula and explain what θ means.\n- Why it introduces no bias: the covariate is independent of assignment.\n- What to do for new users without history (fill missing values with the mean; the gain shrinks).","L0-0.9.5":"Offline evaluation and online experiments play different roles: offline metrics cheaply screen candidate approaches, and A/B tests make the final launch decision. In between, several methods reduce the number of approaches that need online experiments.\n\n**How it works**\n- Offline evaluation: compute AUC, NDCG, etc. on historical data to quickly eliminate weak approaches, though they may not agree with online business metrics.\n- Interleaving: in ranking settings, interleave two models' results for the same user and see which side the user's clicks favor; far more sensitive than A/B and good for quickly comparing ranking models.\n- Off-policy evaluation (OPE): estimate a new policy's online performance from logged data with IPS (inverse propensity scoring) without actually launching it; it relies on the logging policy's propensities and can have high variance.\n- Bandits (e.g. Thompson sampling): replace fixed traffic splits when exploration and exploitation both matter, dynamically shifting more traffic to the better-performing approach to reduce losses, at the cost of more complex statistical inference.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOnline traffic and time are scarce resources. Offline-online disagreement is a common challenge that requires continually calibrating how offline metrics correlate with online ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why offline gains may not show up online: distribution shift, misaligned metrics, feedback loops.\n- Why interleaving is more sensitive.\n- Bandits vs A/B tests: reward vs interpretable inference.","L0-0.9.6":"The framework landscape in 2026: PyTorch is the de facto standard for research and the LLM ecosystem, while TensorFlow / Keras remain common in legacy enterprise systems and on-device deployment. The key in interviews is to show you're not locked into one framework.\n\n**Where things stand (2026-10)**\n- Versions: PyTorch 2.13 (2026-07); TensorFlow 2.21 (2026-03-06), with Keras 3 supporting multiple backends (JAX / TF / PyTorch).\n- Ecosystem: PyTorch has the HF ecosystem, vLLM, torchtune and FSDP2; Google's large-model training is mainly on JAX.\n- Compilation: PyTorch uses `torch.compile` (Dynamo + Inductor), recently adding a CuTeDSL backend and FlexAttention; TF uses XLA and `tf.function`.\n- Deployment: PyTorch covers on-device with `torch.export` + ExecuTorch; TF Serving and TFX are common in legacy systems, with LiteRT and TF.js covering on-device and the browser.\n- Apple Silicon: PyTorch's MPS backend keeps expanding; TF has the tensorflow-metal plugin.\n\n**How to answer \"which one do you use?\"**\nChoose PyTorch for new projects, citing the ecosystem, the hiring pool and the LLM toolchain; also show that you can read and maintain legacy TF / Keras code and know the SavedModel, TF Serving and LiteRT deployment paths. When a JD lists both, what the interviewer usually wants to confirm is your ability to migrate, not API details.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Don't disparage TF; stress choosing by scenario.\n- Know that `tf.lite` has been migrated to LiteRT.","L0-0.9.7":"Classical ML system design questions (recommendation, risk control, forecasting, etc.) have a general answer framework that keeps your answer structurally complete and complements the LLM system design framework.\n\n**The eight-step framework**\n1. Business goals and constraints: what are we optimizing? What are the latency, QPS, interpretability and compliance requirements?\n2. Problem framing: classification, ranking or regression? How are labels defined? How delayed are they?\n3. Data and features: data sources, entities, time-window aggregates, online / offline consistency.\n4. From baseline to model: rules → LR → GBDT → deep models, moving forward only when each step pays off.\n5. Offline evaluation: which metrics, time-based splits, slicing by population.\n6. Serving architecture: online feature store, model serving, degradation and fallbacks.\n7. Online experiments and monitoring: A/B tests, data and prediction drift, backfilling metrics once labels arrive.\n8. Iteration loop: retraining cadence, active learning, human-review feedback.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nInterviewers value reasoning that starts from the business and proactively discusses trade-offs, not reciting model names. The first two steps drive every later choice: the latency budget caps model complexity, and label delay affects evaluation and retraining.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Clarify constraints before diving in; don't open with a model.\n- Online / offline feature inconsistency (training-serving skew) is a frequent follow-up.\n- Give quantifiable acceptance criteria for every step.","L0-0.9.8":"Real-time credit card fraud detection is the archetypal classical ML system design question. The core challenge is balancing fraud losses against the cost of false declines under a strict latency budget.\n\n**Architecture**\nTransaction request → authorization gateway → feature service (online: velocity features computed with Redis / Flink; offline: historical profiles in the feature store) → scoring service (GBDT main model + rule fallback) → decision engine (approve / decline / step-up verification / manual review). Events flow into the offline data warehouse; chargeback / dispute labels flow back weeks later and, together with review outcomes, drive periodic retraining and drift monitoring.\n\n**Key trade-offs**\n- The P99 latency budget (e.g. under 50ms) means only GBDT and precomputed features are viable; no online calls to large models.\n- Set thresholds by business cost (fraud amount vs customer churn from false declines) rather than F1; use amount-weighted recall as the primary metric.\n- Blocking causes selection bias, so a small amount of random pass-through is needed to supplement labels.\n- Declined transactions must come with reasons (top SHAP features).\n- Graph features such as shared devices and merchants are computed offline and used as inputs.\n- LLMs fit as reviewer assistants (summarizing cases, drafting investigation notes), not in the real-time scoring path.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Proactively cover four points: latency budget, label delay, cost-based thresholds, drift monitoring.\n- Explain why random pass-through is needed.","L0-0.9.9":"A trend in 2025–2026 is foundation models moving into classical ML territory: pretrained large models have appeared for tabular data, time series and recommendation, yet GBDT remains the production workhorse.\n\n**Representative progress**\n- Tabular: TabPFN v2 (2025-01, Nature) is pretrained on synthetic data and competes with tuned GBDT on small-to-medium tables with zero tuning; TabPFN-2.5 (2025-11) supports up to 50k rows and 2,000 columns and leads on TabArena.\n- Time series: TimesFM and Chronos offer zero-shot forecasting; TimesFM-3 natively supports multivariate inputs and covariates.\n- Recommendation: Kuaishou's OneRec (2025-06) replaces the multi-stage cascade with an end-to-end generative model; the paper says it has launched and serves about 25% of QPS.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe claim that \"tabular data means GBDT\" is starting to loosen, but GBDT still dominates thanks to low cost, low latency, interpretability and a mature ecosystem. TabPFN-style models predict in a single forward pass via in-context learning, so their scale is limited by context length. A sensible answer: use foundation models for zero-shot baselines and cold start, and GBDT as the mainstay when data is plentiful and latency matters.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Name one representative model per domain and its positioning.\n- When asked \"do we still need GBDT?\", answer by scenario.\n- How generative recommendation differs from the traditional retrieval-ranking cascade.","L0-0.9.10":"When plugging traditional ML prediction models (e.g. risk scores, sales forecasts) into an LLM workflow, the principle is: the model owns the numbers, the LLM handles routing and explanation, so the LLM never \"makes up\" values.\n\n**How it works**\n- Wrap the model as a tool with a typed interface; the LLM decides when to call it and explains the result, but the numbers always come from the model.\n- The tool returns the prediction, an uncertainty estimate or calibrated probability, and input-quality flags; prompt the LLM to say so explicitly when confidence is low rather than guess.\n- For noisy data, put data validation and cleaning before the model, not in the prompt.\n- Log every call and evaluate the combined system end to end; keep the model's own offline metrics, and add tests for tool-calling behavior such as bad arguments and out-of-range inputs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLLMs are good at understanding intent and generating explanations but unreliable at numeric computation and consistency; traditional models are measurable and calibratable. This division of labor gets the best of both, at the cost of an extra layer of orchestration and testing. For deterministic, latency-sensitive flows, call rules or services directly without going through an LLM.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- What if the LLM rewrites the numbers the tool returned incorrectly: structured output + validation.\n- When an LLM should not call the model.\n- How to evaluate the combined system end to end.","L0-0.9.11":"This chapter comes with 7 pure-numpy implementations, each with assertion tests, plus a set of hands-on exercises, to turn classical ML knowledge into code you can write from memory.\n\n**Code (code/ml/)**\n- `kmeans.py`: k-means++ initialization, Lloyd iterations, empty-cluster handling.\n- `logistic_regression.py`: stable sigmoid, BCE + L2, with a finite-difference gradient check.\n- `decision_tree_split.py`: Gini / entropy splits and XGBoost gain, cross-checked against brute-force enumeration.\n- `pca.py`: centering + SVD, explained variance ratio.\n- `matrix_factorization.py`: biased Funk-SVD and top-k recommendation.\n- `auc.py`: rank-formula AUC, AP and ECE, verifying that duplicating negatives leaves ROC unchanged while AP drops.\n- `fedavg.py`: weighted aggregation, clipping, pairwise masks, asserting FedSGD equivalence.\n- `run_all.py` runs everything in one go, about 20 seconds locally.\n\n**Hands-on exercises (all doable on a Mac CPU)**\nRewrite auc and kmeans from memory; imbalance experiments (class weight, isotonic calibration); reproduce target-encoding leakage; generate tabular synthetic data with SDV and evaluate it on fidelity / TSTR / DCR; simulate FedAvg with Flower, comparing IID vs Dirichlet(α = 0.1); simulate A/B peeking and CUPED; talk through a real-time fraud detection design.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Hand-coding AUC, k-means and the logistic regression gradient on the spot are frequent questions.\n- Back up your points with experimental numbers (e.g. a peeking false-positive rate of about 19%)."});
